irgsh (2.3.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * An import now replaces the version our repository carries, older or
    newer. reprepro only upgrades on its own and skipped a downgrade with
    "as it has already <newer version>"; the repo worker now removes an
    imported source or binary whose listed version differs before
    injecting it. The exact same version is still skipped, and
    --force-version re-injects even that
  * The repo worker's dependency check installs each imported package as
    name=version, so a downgrade is checked as the version being imported
    rather than the newer copy already in the repository
  * That check also pins every downloaded version above the repository's own,
    so a package depending on an exact version of a sibling being imported
    with it (python3-uno on libreoffice-core) resolves against the sibling
    instead of failing on the newer copy the import replaces. The sandbox
    no longer reads the host's /etc/apt/preferences either
  * Import every binary of a large source package, not just the first line
    of them. apt-cache showsrc wraps the Binary field over several lines,
    and only the first was read, so importing libreoffice fetched 41 of its
    211 binaries - leaving out libreoffice-report-builder-bin, ure, the
    libuno libraries and fonts-opensymbol, and failing the dependency check
    on packages the import was meant to bring. Fixed in both the repo
    worker and the CLI resolver

 -- BlankOn Developers <blankon-dev@googlegroups.com>  Mon, 21 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0700

irgsh (2.3.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The import dependency check now resolves against the repository chief
    names for the target dist (repo.public_url, over /api/v1/repo-info)
    instead of falling back to the maintainer's own apt sources, which do not
    have to describe the distribution being imported into. Without a
    published public_url the check is skipped with a note rather than
    answering against the wrong repository and blocking a good import
  * Fix two false negatives in that check. Every binary of a source package
    is now resolvable, not just the ones named on the command line - an
    import brings all of them anyway, and pinning only the named ones made
    apt report siblings like libstrongswan as uninstallable. And each package
    is tested on its own instead of all at once, so alternatives that declare
    Conflicts on each other (strongswan-charon and charon-systemd) no longer
    fail a check that a repository never asks them to pass. The repo worker's
    pre-injection check gets the same two fixes
  * irgsh-cli import now works out what else has to be imported when a
    package needs something the target repository lacks, shows it as a table
    with binary counts and download size, and asks y/N before queueing
    anything. --yes accepts without prompting. A dependency the target
    already has at an older version is reported as a blocker instead:
    importing a newer libc6 would replace the C library the distribution is
    built on

 -- BlankOn Developers <blankon-dev@googlegroups.com>  Thu, 10 Sep 2026 23:15:00 +0700

irgsh (2.2.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * irgsh-cli import: --dist now names our distribution to import into, the
    way it already does for package and ISO submissions, and the suite to
    import from moves from --dist to a new --source-dist. --repo-dist is
    gone. The flags now pair up: --dist/--component describe our side,
    --source-dist/--source-component theirs
  * The import wire format follows: "dist" is the target and what the job is
    routed on, "sourceDist" the suite imported from. Chief and the repo
    worker still accept the old shape (a payload carrying "targetDist" and no
    "sourceDist"), so an un-upgraded irgsh-cli or chief keeps working; the
    fallback can be dropped once every deployment is past this release
  * The import_jobs table is unchanged: its dist column still holds the suite
    imported from and target_dist ours, so existing rows read correctly

 -- BlankOn Developers <blankon-dev@googlegroups.com>  Thu, 10 Sep 2026 22:05:00 +0700

irgsh (2.1.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New command: irgsh-cli cancel <pipeline-id> stops a queued or running
    package, ISO or import job. Cancellation is a mark in Redis
    (irgsh:cancel:*), so a running worker stops at once and a worker that
    only picks the job up later refuses to start it
  * A cancelled job's commands are killed as a process group, so the whole
    build tree stops rather than just the shell at its root; the builder
    removes the container its docker run left behind
  * The repo stage cannot be cancelled once it is running, on either side:
    chief refuses the request and the repo worker ignores it. Interrupting
    reprepro, during an export above all, can corrupt the repository
    database. An import is interruptible while it downloads and becomes
    uninterruptible just before it injects
  * Chief records the job as CANCELED as soon as it accepts the request, so
    the dashboard stops showing a spinner for it without waiting for the
    worker; CANCELED is a terminal state and is not overwritten afterwards

 -- BlankOn Developers <blankon-dev@googlegroups.com>  Thu, 10 Sep 2026 21:10:00 +0700

irgsh (2.0.4-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Name the distribution and the real component in job notifications: the
    target tag is now [<suite>/<component>], e.g. [verbeek/main] instead of
    [dev], for build, repo and import jobs. The dev/experimental split is a
    suite (<dist>-experimental), which the old tag reported as if it were the
    component; the component now comes from the submission's own component
    field. A job carrying neither (ISO) drops the tag

